WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.830
Thank you all for being here.

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.380
My name is Shannon
Rose McAuliff.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:06.260
I'm the manager of the
Student Arts programs at MIT.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.850
And we're all here today to
kick off the 2020/2021 15K

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.770
Creative Arts Competition.

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:15.200
We'll have a series
of workshops,

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.130
a really thorough application
session with some feedback,

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:20.540
one-on-one mentoring.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.050
And lots of things like pitch
critiques to help everybody,

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.150
no matter where they
are with their venture

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.370
to enable them to succeed.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:32.880
So I'm really excited
to kick things off.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.090
So without further
ado, I'm going

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.330
to introduce Wendy Swart
Grossman and Jen Guillemin

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:41.610
from Creative Re/Frame.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.500
Hello, MIT.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.980
We're so happy to be here.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.230
I am Jen Guillemin.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.630
And I am Wendy Swart Grossman.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:54.040
And we are so honored to be
with you today here at MIT.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.720
And I want to
start with a story.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.870
About the first
entrepreneur I ever met.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.170
Who trusted his
instincts, took on risk,

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:08.040
invested in his employees, and
challenged conventional wisdom

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.260
with humor and brilliance.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:11.970
It was my brother.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.730
The summer my brother,
Martin, was nine years old,

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.550
he had a crazy
successful lemonade stand

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.200
in front of our house.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.200
He made so much
money, it kept him

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.150
in candy bars and comic books
throughout the long winter.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.850
And based on this
early success at nine,

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.000
the next summer, when he
turned 10, he expanded.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.220
His franchised his
lemonade stands.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.250
And he had three of them around
the neighborhood that were

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.710
run by his trusted buddies.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.550
And when he was 11, he branched
out to the local beach.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.820
And he would sell
cans of ice-cold sodas

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.500
to the sunbathers for
a 50% markup per can.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.390
He was so successful,
he was shut down

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:55.890
by the park and rec police.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.710
Because he didn't have
a peddler's license.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.480
But he didn't just go away.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:01.560
Not my brother, man.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.500
He set up his wagon,
full of coolers,

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.720
on the far side of
the street and had

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.009
his staff of middle schoolers
fan the beach, taking orders.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:12.320
Yeah.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.360
Martin delivered.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:19.100
He was a brilliant man, but
not in a conventional way.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.350
In fact, he barely
graduated from high school.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.480
Because he was too
busy with the company

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.740
that he started at age 16.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.830
He had one of these
curious nimble minds,

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:28.850
and he was hungry.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.620
And he saw a hole in the market.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.990
And the floor cleaning
business that he started

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.420
grew to be the largest
floor cleaning, buffing,

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:39.350
and waxing company in Southern
California, called Floor Tech.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.750
He had a fleet of trucks and
hundreds of crew members,

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:46.820
making him a millionaire
by the time he was 26.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.910
There are so many ways to
be creative in starting

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.310
your business, your own venture.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.470
And just as many
reasons why people

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:58.190
feel compelled to put
something new in the world.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:01.670
For my brother, he loved money.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.030
The more, the better.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.510
In fact, he would
iron his dollar bills

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.840
in order to fit more of
them into his wallet.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.390
To him, it was a
fascinating game.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.260
He also loved creating
networks and talking

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:19.250
to people, similar to myself,
and giving his employees

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:22.250
opportunities to change
their lot in life

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:25.100
by offering them real wages.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:29.900
And I'm an entrepreneur myself,
but my bottom line isn't money.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:34.340
I'm motivated by how many
people I can touch, and help,

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:35.870
and organize.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:40.160
My return on investment is
measured, not only by profit,

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:43.490
but on the social return
on investment, known

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:45.410
as the triple bottom line.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.640
Yes, profit.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.020
But people and planet, as well.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.600
While my brother was
busy making his millions,

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.670
I was a political organizer.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:57.730
Tapping into my super
power of connection

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.340
by working on
presidential campaigns.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.040
And then pivoting
to non-profits.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.000
And now I am both an educator,
and co-founder with Jen,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.270
of a creative
consulting startup.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:15.260
I needed to trust
what motivated me.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.250
But enough about me.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:17.480
Enough about my brother.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.790
What we're here for
is to talk about you.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:22.520
What is important
here, is understanding

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:26.690
and tapping into what it
is that motivates you.

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:28.940
Where do you have,
and where do you

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:34.050
find what Jen and I have
coined as your CIIP.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.500
Your care.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:36.750
What do you care about?

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.160
What issue is it
that interests you?

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.770
What ideas do you have?

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.140
And where is your passion?

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.080
Because the more you
understand and trust

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.570
your motivation for
doing what you do,

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.310
the more authentic, the
more connected, and the more

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.350
meaningful your work becomes.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:59.830
And this is what the 15K
Creative Arts Competition here

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:02.000
at MIT is really all about.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.170
It's about giving you the
tools and the incentives

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:09.040
you need to start your
own creative venture.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.000
We know that the best
ventures are those that solve,

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.620
not only real problems, but
are attached to a passion

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:22.440
that you, as the founder, holds.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.110
When I moved to Boston
in the mid '90s,

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.090
I went to a
community arts event.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:30.450
And I met a person who
changed the course of my life.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:32.940
His name was Sidewalk Sam.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:36.240
And he was an artist, a
provocateur, a visionary,

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.650
and the first creative
entrepreneur I ever met.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.840
If you were living in
Boston in the mid '90s,

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.210
you would know exactly the
person that I'm talking about.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.910
Sidewalk Sam was
trained as an artist

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:51.240
and wanted to make his
living being an artist.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.450
He started out as a, kind
of in a traditional way,

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:58.110
of having a studio and
working with galleries.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:05:59.580
But that didn't work for him.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.120
He wanted something else.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.120
So he carved out his own unique
niche as a street artist.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.800
He began using chalk to draw
replicas of famous masterpieces

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.450
on the sidewalks
throughout Boston.

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:16.800
And as his fame grew,
so did his client base

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.160
and corporate sponsors.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:22.640
The streets of Boston
became his Canvas.

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:26.840
In 1995, five years before I
met him, the mayor of Boston

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:30.170
was trying to figure out how to
celebrate the 20th anniversary

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:31.760
of Earth Day.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:34.250
Sidewalk Sam made a proposal.

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:36.330
He said, I know, I have an idea.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.450
Let's shut down the streets--

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:41.300
a major thoroughfare,
[INAUDIBLE] Drive in Boston.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.460
Paint it with
biodegradable paint,

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:45.770
and invite all the people
of Boston to come out

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:48.380
and draw flowers, and
trees, and butterflies.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:52.110
Turning a highway
into an urban meadow.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:55.320
When they looked at him
like he was crazy and asked

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.450
how he thought they
might pay for this,

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.950
he was two steps ahead of them.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:02.890
He had already secured Dunkin'
Donuts as a corporate sponsor.

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:06.520
Thousands of people came
out to the streets that day

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:10.120
and drew pictures
on Storrow Drive.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.570
The genius of Sidewalk
Sam was his ability

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:16.990
to leverage the transformative
power of the arts

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:19.030
to create community
in partnership

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:20.830
with local businesses.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:24.550
His sponsors achieved their
strategic business objectives

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:26.440
while supporting Sidewalks Sam.

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:29.590
And he was able to achieve
his mission of bringing art

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:31.480
to the people.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.360
I should mention, as
a bit of a disclaimer,

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:37.120
that I spent a lot of time with
Sidewalk Sam over the years

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:40.240
because I ended up marrying
his son in the late '90s.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:43.810
And together, we volunteered
at hundreds of events

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:46.890
over the course of two decades.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.710
Throughout his career,
Sidewalk Sam's and events

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:53.160
became more and more
participatory in nature.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:58.220
As he espoused the belief
that everyone is an artist.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:02.150
To him, the arts weren't
rarefied and only

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:03.230
for the elite.

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:07.570
The arts were alive, and they
were for all of us to share.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:09.730
At one festival in
downtown Boston,

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:11.980
I volunteered to hand out chalk.

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:15.490
A man was walking past,
and he reluctantly

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:19.090
agreed to take a piece of
chalk and draw on the sidewalk.

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:20.920
However, when he
started drawing,

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:22.990
his whole demeanor changed.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:25.360
Usually people draw
for maybe five minutes.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:28.090
He ended up staying and
drawing for a full hour.

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.220
And when he returned
the chalk to me,

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:32.846
he had tears in his eyes.

00:08:32.846 --> 00:08:35.289
He explained to me that
he worked his whole life

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:36.100
in insurance.

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:39.580
And he loved to draw as a
kid, and he only that day

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:42.020
remembered how
much he missed it.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.270
Something opened
up in him that day.

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:46.070
And that was the
magic of Sidewalk Sam.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:49.340
And his form of
entrepreneurship was

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:53.120
to create environments that
change hearts and minds.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:55.980
Sidewalk Sam made his
living doing what he loved,

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:59.600
which is not easy in the arts,
and his return on investment

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.730
was collective joy.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:04.190
He changed lives by
helping people unlock

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:06.140
their personal creativity.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:08.180
And he certainly influenced me.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:10.220
Because I became
an entrepreneur,

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:13.160
working within a University
to create programs and courses

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:14.390
for art students.

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:19.560
So they, too, could find ways to
unlock their creative purpose.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:21.990
Whether you relate
more to Martin,

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:25.200
the millionaire story,
or Sidewalk Sam's model

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:28.590
of the importance of putting
something out in the world

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:32.250
that taps into connection
and creativity.

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:33.570
It really doesn't matter.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.850
Because they're both
incredibly important,

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:38.740
and they're both equally valid.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:43.590
There is room at the table for
all the reasons people show up.

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:46.290
And are motivated to create.

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:51.090
And there really is no better
place to explore this than MIT.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:56.520
Math, science, technology,
but also arts resources.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.330
Like the one that this very
15K Creative Arts Competition

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:01.500
represents.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.960
Through the arts
at MIT programming.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:08.710
What Jen and I have found,
from working in the innovation

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:12.250
and entrepreneurship
spaces, is the importance

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:15.055
of this type of
cross-disciplinary thinking.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:23.500
This all starts with having
that diversity of ideas.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:27.740
And the skill-sets
to get it out there.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:31.030
So while, right now, you may
just have a hint of an idea,

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:33.190
it's crucial to say it aloud.

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:34.730
And to talk with
others about it.

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:37.360
And to think broadly
about what skills

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:42.130
will be needed to put this idea
that's hatching into action.

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:44.470
Take an inventory
of yourself and be

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:47.800
incredibly honest of the skills
you come to the table with.

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:51.890
And what it is you need,
that you don't have.

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:55.280
Then, finding others
that share your big goal.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:56.630
Your big dream.

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:59.600
But come at it from really
different entry points,

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:02.300
as this will only make
your finished project

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:04.580
that much better.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:07.970
For example, Jen and I have
crazy different backgrounds.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:12.620
So Jen has this incredibly rich
career in both higher education

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:13.940
and the arts world.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:17.840
And I come from a political
background, as I mentioned.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:20.660
But together, we
started a company.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:24.950
But we also knew that we
needed help from other folks.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:28.400
So we reached out to the
Small Business Development

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:31.310
Administration in
downtown Boston.

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:34.670
And met with their volunteer
teams of lawyers and business

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:35.750
advisors.

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:40.530
And then we hired a designer to
get our website up and running.

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:43.400
And now, we employ
other incredible people

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:46.520
who help us with the pieces
that they love and are really

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:49.790
good at, including
social media, database

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:52.390
management, and marketing.

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:55.110
So as you're thinking
about your venture that

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:57.630
might be brewing in
the back of your head,

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:03.120
think about what makes for
a powerful combination.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.090
What are your superpowers?

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:06.230
And be honest.

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:13.240
And who can help fill in the
holes with their superpowers.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.510
But we need to get real.

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:18.640
We need to really
talk and address

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.670
the elephant in the room
and, unfortunately, it's

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:23.950
more like a whole
herd of elephants.

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:27.700
We are nine days away from
the most important election

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:30.350
in a hundred or more years.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:35.930
220,000 people have died in
the US alone from Covid-19.

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:40.490
And over one million have
died across our shared planet.

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:44.540
Disparity between the haves
and the have-nots is huge.

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:46.910
And unfortunately, it's growing.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:49.550
And, of course, this
is all playing out

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:55.280
with the backdrop of a
world that is on fire.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:59.840
Other than that, Mrs. Lincoln,
how did you enjoy the play?

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:01.250
It's nutty.

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:04.520
But we're to tell you
that there is hope.

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:06.770
And there is inspiration.

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:12.800
And what we are finding is that
creativity is alive and well.

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:18.140
Human beings are programmed
to be in community, to live,

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:21.680
to inspire, to pivot and grow.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:25.310
And this provides all of
us with the incredible hope

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:30.690
and inspiration we all need
to get ourselves out of bed.

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:34.110
We, as a nation, are
turning to the arts.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:39.930
And to creative problem-solvers
as our salve, our balm,

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:41.790
our hope.

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:45.090
One of the projects that Jen and
I work on at Creative Re/Frame

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:47.490
is called "100 Conversations."

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:51.390
Launched in the summer
of 2019, the two of us

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:55.020
would jump on our bikes
and tool around Boston,

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:58.050
meeting with individual,
inspirational creative

00:13:58.050 --> 00:14:00.690
practitioners, artists,
and thought leaders

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:03.780
in order to help build
a culture of creativity

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:05.850
around their shared stories.

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:09.660
Making connections and
building community.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:12.870
This, of course, is now
morphed into socially distanced

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:15.450
conversations and Zoom calls.

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:19.470
And we are documenting how
these same incredible arts

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:25.210
leaders are pivoting, thinking
broader, and be, in many ways,

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:27.660
even more impactful.

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:30.180
One of these conversations
was with the Arts

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:33.000
at MIT's very own Shannon Rose.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:37.090
Who, in addition to adding
her brilliance to MIT,

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:41.190
she's a singer, and the
co-founder of Festina.

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:44.670
Bringing gorgeous,
uplifting church music

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:47.530
to congregations
across New England.

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:51.640
Thanks to COVID, they have
pivoted to a unique niche

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:54.440
and have had thousands
of hits for a piece

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:58.960
they have done set in
the CDC COVID Guidelines

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:00.670
to Liturgical Music.

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:03.800
Check out the chat
box for the link.

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:06.800
In addition to Festina,
we've had conversations

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:11.090
with arts administrators,
entrepreneurs, muralists,

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:12.540
and musicians.

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:17.030
And while our current
situation is less than ideal,

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:22.550
to paraphrase Toni Morrison,
"This is precisely the time

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:24.740
that creatives go to work."

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:26.630
There's no time for despair.

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:28.430
No time for self-pity.

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:30.500
No need for silence.

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:33.140
No room for fear.

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:37.190
We speak, we write,
we do language.

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:41.370
That is how civilizations heal.

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:43.710
For 10 years, Wendy
and I have been

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:46.380
teaching courses on
cultural entrepreneurship

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:49.860
and the creative economy to
millennials and Gen Z students.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:52.110
Initially, our goal was
to provide our students

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:54.780
with basic business skills to
help them build sustainable

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:56.690
careers in the arts.

00:15:56.690 --> 00:15:58.640
But what we found
was that students

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:02.910
from across the University,
business, computer science,

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:06.790
even engineering, were signing
up for our classes too.

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:08.950
Even if they didn't
plan on going

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:11.920
into the creative professions,
they were drawn to the class.

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:13.810
And we wondered why.

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:15.890
What we learned,
that these students

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:18.670
craved opportunities
to be creative,

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:20.770
and to work across disciplines.

00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:24.160
And think of new ways that
they can both make money

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:27.053
while doing something that
was meaningful to them.

00:16:27.053 --> 00:16:28.720
We were teaching
something they were not

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:31.360
getting in some of
their other courses,

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:35.620
reminding us once again that
arts and creativity are really

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:39.490
inherent to what it
means to be human.

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:43.450
Our class helps students
create and launch

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:46.960
ventures that reside at
the intersection of arts

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:51.850
and culture, business and
technology, and social impact.

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:55.060
And we know that your
generation has the capacity

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:58.410
to re-imagine better
jobs of the future.

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:01.470
And what better place to
explore this convergence

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:05.599
of arts and technology,
than right here at MIT.

00:17:05.599 --> 00:17:09.349
At Creative Re/Frame,
we have a saying.

00:17:09.349 --> 00:17:13.550
And it goes like this,
"Make like an artist.

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:16.589
Care like an activist.

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:19.170
Strategize like an entrepreneur.

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:22.270
And implement like
an organizer."

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:25.829
So as you begin your journey
in the 15K Creative Arts

00:17:25.829 --> 00:17:31.890
Competition, we leave you
with four key takeaways.

00:17:31.890 --> 00:17:33.150
Make.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:36.570
Trust your creative vision.

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:38.700
Don't be afraid to
think differently.

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.530
Invite disruption.

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:43.830
Find a need, and think
about your unique approach

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:46.190
to meeting that need.

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:47.990
Think about your CIIP.

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:52.710
As Wendy mentioned earlier,
your care, idea, issue, passion.

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:56.030
Because it is your
CIIP, that is your fuel.

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:58.490
It is what will help you get
through sleepless nights,

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:00.650
rejection, and the
grueling work that

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:04.450
comes hand-in-hand with
creating a startup.

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:08.560
For Martin, he knew exactly what
people wanted on that hot beach

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:10.090
during the summer.

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:14.560
For Sidewalk Sam, he recognized
the need for creativity

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:16.810
in everyday life.

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:19.960
At Creative Re/Frame, we
care about integrating

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:22.360
arts and innovation spaces.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:26.340
What is your creative vision.

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:27.540
Care.

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:29.490
Get other people excited.

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:32.400
And get other people to
care about your vision.

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:35.100
Find others who want
to share your passion,

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:37.080
and build your team.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:41.750
People are attracted to
purpose and a clear vision.

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:46.130
The research shows that most
people find meaning and purpose

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:49.340
when they are part of something
bigger than themselves.

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:52.240
So, with that in mind,
how might your venture

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:55.030
consider the triple bottom line.

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:57.850
People, planet, and profit.

00:18:57.850 --> 00:19:01.150
By attaching yourself
to a greater purpose,

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:03.040
people are more
likely to join you

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:05.290
in implementing that vision.

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:07.810
Whether it was Martin's
team of helpers combing

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:12.040
that beach or Sidewalk Sam's
participants, who rediscovered

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:14.110
art after many years.

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:17.050
These are the things that you
could be doing when you're

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:19.240
CIIP is steeped in meaning.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:21.880
You can find and
build ventures that

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:24.940
not only help other
people's lives,

00:19:24.940 --> 00:19:28.370
but also offer you
deep satisfaction.

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:29.780
Strategize.

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:32.860
Seek and build excellence.

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:35.590
Build operational
excellence around your idea.

00:19:35.590 --> 00:19:39.190
Put together a solid business
model that you feel proud of

00:19:39.190 --> 00:19:41.110
and that meets a need.

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:43.930
Addressing not only the
return on the investment,

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:47.740
but also the social
return on investment.

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:50.280
And finally, implement.

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:52.920
Use the vast resources
that are available

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:55.470
right here at MIT,
and across Boston.

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:57.930
There will be workshops
and programs designed

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:01.350
to help you build and
amplify your venture

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:04.380
so you can bring your creative
vision into the spotlight.

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:06.570
Take advantage of all of it.

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:08.490
As you will carry
these skills with you

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:10.550
for the rest of your life.

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:12.710
Throughout the
entire process, we

00:20:12.710 --> 00:20:14.990
remind you to
remember the values

00:20:14.990 --> 00:20:17.030
of thinking like an artist.

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:20.810
Remember the power of
improvisation, empathy,

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:24.080
social imagination,
and resourcefulness.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:26.900
In her book, "The
Culture of Possibility,"

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:29.750
Arlene Goldbard writes,
"We need to learn

00:20:29.750 --> 00:20:33.170
to see like the most
committed and skilled artists.

00:20:33.170 --> 00:20:38.120
Eyes wide open, taking it all
in, turning away from nothing.

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:41.120
Cultivating empathy
and imagination.

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:44.980
Taking risks admitting
mistakes and persevering."

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:48.130
In essence, my friends, the
arts and entrepreneurship

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:49.780
are really not that different.

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:53.660
Both rely on storytelling to
express compelling narratives.

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:56.620
Both are about taking
risks and making mistakes

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:58.740
and persevering.

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:01.560
Whatever your
entrepreneurial journey is,

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:04.590
and whatever story
got you here today,

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:08.440
we are here to say
yes, go for it.

00:21:08.440 --> 00:21:10.000
You've got this.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:13.740
There is so much hope and
potential and opportunity

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:17.190
at the intersection of
arts and technology.

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:20.280
And the 15K Creative
Arts Competition

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:23.850
is the perfect
launching pad for you.

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:27.180
We commend you as you
set forth on your journey

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:28.980
and we look forward
to seeing the seeds

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:31.260
that you plant today, and
see how they grow over

00:21:31.260 --> 00:21:33.120
the course of the months ahead.

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:35.700
Your work will
help to re-imagine

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:40.280
the jobs, the products, and
the services of the future.

00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:41.620
Thank you for joining us.

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:44.320
And thank you Arts
at MIT for inviting

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:48.520
Creative Re/Frame to help
launch the 15K Creative Arts

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:50.190
Competition.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:51.970
Thank you both so much.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:54.600
That was fantastic.

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:56.980
Also your technology
is so impressive.

00:21:56.980 --> 00:22:01.644
This is the best Zoom that
I've seen in eight months.

00:22:01.644 --> 00:22:04.190
Round of applause
for the technician

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:07.410
behind the kitchen table!

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:09.340
Beautifully done, everybody.

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:12.510
So that was full of some
really fantastic insights.

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:14.310
And a lot of motivation.

00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:17.220
I'm curious to know if anybody
has any questions for Wendy

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:18.000
and Jen.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:21.510
Or if Wendy and Jen have
any questions for others.

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:23.760
They sort of like to turn
these things upside down,

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:27.870
so we're going to do
a 360 conversation

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:29.280
before we take our break.

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:35.390
Well we would like to invite
some of the people here

00:22:35.390 --> 00:22:39.550
today to share your
creative vision with us,

00:22:39.550 --> 00:22:41.133
if we have anybody.

00:22:41.133 --> 00:22:43.550
Jen and I have been working
in this field for a long time.

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:46.040
And working with students
just like all of you

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:48.950
to help you take these little
ideas, and help them launch.

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:53.330
And we're so curious who's
out there in the Zoom-verse

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:59.160
to see how we can help you
as you move through it all.

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:01.550
Anything bubbling out there?

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:05.180
I'll also comment that, if
you need a little time to sort

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:07.520
of marinate on everything
that we just discussed,

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:09.620
our third session
will also include

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:11.960
sort of the opportunity
for conversation

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:14.480
with really wonderfully
established entrepreneurs

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:15.620
and educators.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:17.600
I know that we have
some people in here who

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:19.080
are studying entrepreneurship.

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:22.130
So I'd be curious to see
what the takeaways were,

00:23:22.130 --> 00:23:25.990
or what's excited
you about this.

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:27.400
And we're kind of open books.

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:28.910
I'll talk about anything.

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:29.410
[INAUDIBLE]

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:29.910
Yeah.

00:23:29.910 --> 00:23:32.390
Yeah, whatever.

00:23:32.390 --> 00:23:32.960
Let's see.

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:36.290
I mean this was all
really motivating,

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:38.600
and I've got a question.

00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:41.900
It can all seem sort
of like scaling Everest

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:43.800
when you finally got
these great ideas,

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:46.420
and you finally got
all of this energy.

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:49.500
It's an awfully
big uphill climb.

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:52.430
So what are the smallest, or
sort of most important steps,

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:54.170
that you would say
will help people

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:55.490
to catalyze their success.

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:03.440
So what I usually do, is
it's all about ripping off

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:04.610
your blinders.

00:24:04.610 --> 00:24:08.460
Going through the world and
starting to be, what I call,

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:10.010
as being awake and aware.

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:14.210
Paying attention to what's
working, what's not working.

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:16.700
Where is there like an
interesting hole in the market?

00:24:16.700 --> 00:24:18.410
What do you like to do?

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:21.710
So it really kind of depends.

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:23.450
Sometimes you start
with a problem.

00:24:23.450 --> 00:24:25.490
Sometimes you
start with an idea.

00:24:25.490 --> 00:24:28.040
Sometimes you start
because you're excited.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:30.810
And sometimes you start
because you're pissed off.

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:33.650
There's so many different
fabulous organizations

00:24:33.650 --> 00:24:36.290
that have been popping
up that are student led.

00:24:36.290 --> 00:24:38.750
That was because they realized
that the world was on fire

00:24:38.750 --> 00:24:42.050
and who's going to start.

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:44.240
So it's really kind
of paying attention

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:45.890
to both sides of the coin.

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:48.170
And really, it starts
with that whole inventory

00:24:48.170 --> 00:24:50.600
of what skills do I have.

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:51.800
What am I good at.

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:57.450
Also paying attention
too to oh, I like this.

00:24:57.450 --> 00:24:59.390
I want more of this.

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:03.470
What is this for you.

00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:05.170
Anything to add?

00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:07.870
I think you I think did
a good job covering.

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:09.430
I think, also,
there are people who

00:25:09.430 --> 00:25:12.340
have these crossover interests
that are really interesting.

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:14.892
And I think at a
place like MIT, that

00:25:14.892 --> 00:25:16.100
is something that you'll see.

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:18.725
So you'll have somebody who has
this background in engineering,

00:25:18.725 --> 00:25:21.040
but they're also really
passionate about music.

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:24.040
And so to think about what's
happening at that intersection,

00:25:24.040 --> 00:25:26.980
and what might you bring to it
that is so unique about you.

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:28.660
Because you have
these two interests

00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:31.280
that collide in really
interesting ways.

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:35.020
So I think to be
really aware of that.

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:36.610
We think of the
arts as something

00:25:36.610 --> 00:25:39.610
that can just be a source
of inspiration internally,

00:25:39.610 --> 00:25:42.850
but it also could be part
of your business idea.

00:25:42.850 --> 00:25:47.140
And that could launch
you into a new direction.

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:52.020
And what we love is that
whole cross-disciplinary mess.

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:54.660
And sometimes, if we
were together in person,

00:25:54.660 --> 00:25:57.300
we would be all be wearing name
tags with different colored

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:58.170
dots on them.

00:25:58.170 --> 00:26:01.550
One would be red, "I have
an idea, ask me about it."

00:26:01.550 --> 00:26:04.100
Another one would
be blue, saying,

00:26:04.100 --> 00:26:07.150
"I've got a lot of cool skills
and I want to help you."

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:09.660
So it's like finding
those other people.

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:12.000
Sometimes we do
have the idea and we

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:13.260
don't know what to do next.

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:15.940
Sometimes we want to
work with somebody else,

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:18.090
but we don't have the idea.

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:21.463
So that's the trick, is being
able to find each other.

00:26:21.463 --> 00:26:23.880
Because you don't want to do
it alone, because that's like

00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:27.410
boring and lonely.

00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:30.080
Those are all really these
are excellent points.

00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:32.180
Especially about the
necessity of team building

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:34.470
in partnership.

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:37.040
Yeah, definitely and we had
a question based on that.

00:26:37.040 --> 00:26:40.690
So thinking about those
kind of key partnerships.

00:26:40.690 --> 00:26:43.740
How do you know when you're
ready for a partnership?

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:47.980
And how do you go about finding
those kind of key partners?

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:49.360
You're ready for a partnership.

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:50.470
You always want a partner.

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:51.380
Don't do it alone.

00:26:51.380 --> 00:26:52.980
That's a big red flag.

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:54.585
I'm like, oh I've
got this great idea.

00:26:54.585 --> 00:26:55.960
I'm going to do
it all by myself.

00:26:55.960 --> 00:26:58.400
And I'm like, oh, cringe,
cringe, cringe, cringe.

00:26:58.400 --> 00:26:58.900
No.

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:00.950
You always need help.

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:02.532
And so that was the first part.

00:27:02.532 --> 00:27:04.240
What was the second
part of the question.

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:04.540
I don't know.

00:27:04.540 --> 00:27:06.832
I just got excited about
finding somebody to work with.

00:27:06.832 --> 00:27:09.070
Well I really like
your question.

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:11.480
Because I think it happens
in a lot of different ways.

00:27:11.480 --> 00:27:14.680
Sometimes people really
unite over shared interest.

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:16.990
Sometimes you just have
chemistry with somebody.

00:27:16.990 --> 00:27:19.380
And then you're like, how
can we do something together.

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:24.915
So as just a little back story,
when Wendy and I first met,

00:27:24.915 --> 00:27:26.290
we didn't really
know each other.

00:27:26.290 --> 00:27:27.665
And we just went
out for a drink.

00:27:27.665 --> 00:27:28.660
And we started talking.

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:33.460
I was asked to propose a course.

00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:36.080
And because I had such a
great conversation with Wendy,

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:37.540
I was like I want to co-teach.

00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:40.040
I bet we'd be really good
co-teaching together.

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:42.070
And so we started that.

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:44.230
And so it was just
because we had a great--

00:27:44.230 --> 00:27:46.060
we had--

00:27:46.060 --> 00:27:47.250
We like to play together.

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:50.060
We try to figure out other
ways that we can play.

00:27:50.060 --> 00:27:53.530
And I want to be fun to teach.

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:56.120
Because I'm really a
frustrated stand up comic.

00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:58.450
And I thought, then
you have people

00:27:58.450 --> 00:27:59.980
who have to come to your class.

00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:02.147
And then that's like, ooh,
a mandatory [INAUDIBLE]..

00:28:02.147 --> 00:28:04.570
And I was like, how cool.

00:28:04.570 --> 00:28:06.700
But I had never
really talked before.

00:28:06.700 --> 00:28:09.270
And I had done workshops
or whatever, but never

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:10.510
a full-on course.

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:12.970
And I wasn't afraid to say yes.

00:28:12.970 --> 00:28:16.210
I'm like, oh, propose a
University course to be you?

00:28:16.210 --> 00:28:18.190
Because that's where we started.

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:21.918
And that's like
holding your breath.

00:28:21.918 --> 00:28:23.710
Holding your breath
and saying, "Yeah, I'll

00:28:23.710 --> 00:28:26.470
do that, even though I've
never done it before."

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:27.730
Write a syllabus?

00:28:27.730 --> 00:28:29.620
How the heck do you do that?

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:34.240
And you do a lot of what's
called, well in the art

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:36.190
world, it's called inspiration.

00:28:36.190 --> 00:28:38.990
But between you and I, we
just steal each other's stuff,

00:28:38.990 --> 00:28:39.490
right.

00:28:39.490 --> 00:28:41.980
We steal it, but
then we remake it.

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:43.090
We are inspired.

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:44.620
But you take other
people's stuff,

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:46.690
and then you remake
it in a way that

00:28:46.690 --> 00:28:49.208
makes a lot more sense
for your audience.

00:28:49.208 --> 00:28:50.500
But you got to start somewhere.

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:52.510
Start with some
kind of a framework.

00:28:52.510 --> 00:28:53.590
And that's OK.

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:56.165
You can do that.

00:28:56.165 --> 00:28:56.665
All right.

00:28:56.665 --> 00:28:58.240
That's legal.

00:28:58.240 --> 00:28:59.180
That is legal.

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:01.710
That is OK and encouraged.

00:29:01.710 --> 00:29:03.160
I'm really delighted with this.

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:05.560
I want to congratulate you
both for your successes.

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:07.960
And thank you so much
for your generosity

00:29:07.960 --> 00:29:10.380
in sharing your insights.

